Now I see the issue! Look at the expand string section:

```c
if (NODE_TYPE(body) == NODE_STRING) {
    if (!IS_INFINITE_REPEAT(qn->lower) && qn->lower == qn->upper &&
        qn->lower > 1 && qn->lower <= EXPAND_STRING_MAX_LENGTH) {
      int len = NODE_STRING_LEN(body);
      if (len * qn->lower <= EXPAND_STRING_MAX_LENGTH) {
        int i, n = qn->lower;
        node_conv_to_str_node(node, body);
        for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
          r = node_str_node_cat(node, body);
          if (r != 0) return r;
        }
        onig_node_free(body);
        return r;
      }
    }
  }
```

After `node_conv_to_str_node(node, body)`, `node` is converted from a quantifier to a string node. Then `body` is freed. But `qn` is a pointer to `QUANT_(node)` which was captured at the beginning of the function. After `node_conv_to_str_node`, `node` is no longer a quantifier, so `qn` is a dangling pointer!

But wait, `node_conv_to_str_node` does `xmemset(node, 0, sizeof(*node))` which zeros out the entire node. So `qn` would be pointing to zeroed memory. But the function returns immediately after `onig_node_free(body)`, so `qn` is not accessed after this point. This is safe.

